# Technical Stack

## Core Technologies

- Docker as the primary technology for building container images
- Multi-platform Docker builds (linux/amd64, linux/arm64, darwin/amd64, darwin/arm64)
- Initial focus on Ubuntu-based images with planned expansion to Arch, Alpine, and macOS
- Containerized development environment using DevContainers

## Development Tools

- VS Code as the recommended IDE with DevContainers extension
- NVIM available inside containers as an alternative editor (configured with Lua)
- ZSH with Zim framework as the default shell environment
- Bash for all shell scripts to ensure maximum compatibility
- XDG Base Directory Specification adherence for configuration files

## Build System

- Mise to centralize all build commands, tests, and CI operations
- Multi-stage Docker builds to minimize final image size
- BuildKit enabled for efficient Docker builds
- Docker Compose for testing container configurations

## Quality Assurance

- Hadolint for Dockerfile linting and best practices validation
- ShellCheck for validating shell scripts
- Container structure tests for verifying image contents
- Integration tests for each supported distribution

## Infrastructure & CI/CD

- GitHub Actions for automated testing and deployment pipeline
- DockerHub for storing and distributing images
- Container Registry scanning integration
- Semantic versioning for image tags
- Automated generation of container documentation

## Security

- Automatic vulnerability scanning in CI pipeline

## Source Control

- Conventional Commits for clear commit history
- Branch protection rules for main/master branches
- Pull requests required for all changes
- Automated PR validation through CI

## Documentation

- Markdown for all documentation
- Automated README generation based on image capabilities
- Standardized labels for all container images
- Clear examples for using the base images
